  "methodology": {
    "daffi": "4.0 defaults: runtime=threads, workers=1, no TLS. MSGPACK is the default codec. OPAQUE ships the buffer unchanged. The router row is Client \u2192 Router \u2192 worker (two TCP hops).",
    "others": "Same host, loopback TCP, no TLS, server in its own process. gRPC uses a registered unary method, no per-call deadline, retries off, HTTP proxy off. Sequential gRPC is tuned for latency with one server thread. Concurrent gRPC uses the throughput target; the sync server pool is at least the client count, and the aio row is grpc.aio with one channel per client. ZeroMQ sequential is REQ/REP. ZeroMQ concurrent is one DEALER socket per client against one ROUTER. nng is req/rep. RPyC uses ThreadedServer and brine. Pyro5 uses its default serpent codec. ZeroRPC uses its default msgpack-over-ZeroMQ server.",
    "timing": "Warmup is outside the timer. calls/s and avg_latency_us are wall-clock of a loop that does not timestamp each call. p50_us and p99_us are from a second pass and are omitted for payload and client rows. Published values are medians; calls_per_s_min and calls_per_s_max are the repeat spread. goodput_mib_s counts the payload twice (request and response) and divides by 1024*1024.",
    "do_not_mix": "raw-bytes and object rows are different amounts of work. RPyC on the client chart serializes the bytes with brine; Daffi OPAQUE, gRPC raw, and ZeroMQ do not. The clients group is its own harness (a thread barrier and 1,000 calls per client). Use it to compare a framework with itself at 1, 8, and 32 clients. The one-in-flight latency table is the bytes and object groups.",
    "order_json_bytes": 2019,
    "sequential_calls": 10000,
    "repeats": 3
  },
